# [1].map(double); step0(), which hands out bare0.bind(), Function.prototype.call.bind(throughCall0, undefined), Function.prototype.apply.bind(throughApply0, undefined),
# Reflect.apply.bind(undefined, throughReflectApply0, undefined, [1]), Reflect.construct.bind(undefined, throughReflectConstruct0, [1]) and step1, which does the same with its own five functions and step2, up to step4
# The function of the library that runs for Array.prototype.map resolves a promise of a constructor that it knows nothing about, which constructs that constructor.
# Only whoever was handed a step can call it, so the bound functions of each step are handed out later than those of the step before. Whoever holds a bound function can call it:
# all twenty-five functions are entered, whichever of the bound functions are handed out at the time when the library constructs.
# bound-call-apply-and-reflect-enter-their-targets-while-library-code-constructs-an-unknown-constructor.ir with another order of the rules: the answer is the same.
